name bvr-duel-1
timeout 320

# THE DUEL: two intercept pilots, the same code, pointed at each other. Nothing is scripted on either
# side — no briefed release, no briefed chaff, no briefed manoeuvre. Each jet is given what a controller
# gives a fighter (a vector and a master-arm call) and then decides everything else for itself out of
# its own radar, its own warning receiver and its own fire control.
#
# THIS IS THE MISSION THE WHOLE INTERCEPT PHASE EXISTS FOR, and it is also the one whose result is not
# designed. Both jets will detect, both will lock, both will shoot, and from the first lock onwards each
# of them is inside the other's decision loop: a lock spike on the receiver is a warning, and the rule
# systems/FBPilot follows is that a track spike is worth turning for only once this jet's own shot no
# longer needs supporting. Two aircraft applying that rule to each other is exactly the mutual-kill
# problem of a head-on BVR merge, and whatever comes out of it is the answer.
#
# THE ONE ASYMMETRY IS MISSION DATA, NOT CODE: viper starts 1,000 m higher and 50 kt faster than bandit.
# That is the classic BVR advantage — a round launched higher and faster inherits both, and Raero is an
# integration that starts from the launcher's own velocity (modules/f16/FBF16FireControl) — and it is
# introduced deliberately so the run is a fight rather than a mirror. A perfectly symmetric setup would
# produce two identical timelines and prove nothing about the decision rules.
#
# THE GEOMETRY: 99 km apart, head-on, closing at roughly 700 m/s. Both start well outside the APG-68's
# 40 nm search gate, so both runs begin with a real search phase.
#
# WHAT TO READ, in BOTH telemetry files side by side (telemetry.csv = viper, telemetry_bandit.csv):
#   eng_detect_s / eng_lock_s   who saw whom first, and who spent the first lock — the lock is what
#                               puts a symbol on the other jet's scope, so the first one to lock is the
#                               first one to be seen coming
#   eng_shot_s / eng_shot_nm    who shot first and from where
#   eng_threat_s / eng_react_s  when each receiver lit up and how long its pilot took to answer
#   eng_state                   the two state machines interleaved: attack/support against defend
#   eng_support_f / eng_pitbull whether each shot was supported to its seeker's own activation, which is
#                               the difference between a launch and a kill
#   DETONATION / MISS           what actually arrived, measured on the published poses by the runner's
#                               own proximity fuze — never by the weapon's opinion of itself
# There is no damage model, so neither jet stops flying when it is hit. The verdict is therefore read
# from the miss distances and the two timelines, not from who is left; the run ends at its timeout
# (exit 3) like every other engagement mission, for the same reason (a fight has no waypoint).

unit viper
  team friendly
  module f16
  spawn 46.90000 6.40000 9000 90.0 450
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 70
  set datalink off
  set fcr_mode crm
  set store 3 aim120
  set store 7 aim120
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set cmds_mode man                        # the pilot throws over the CMS switch, HOTAS latency
  set cmds_program 1                       # PRGM 1 = BREAK LOCK, 4 cartridges in ~1.1 s
  set cmds_chaff 60
  set cmds_flare 60
  set task intercept
  wp 46.90000 8.35000 9000 450             # the vector: threat bearing 090

unit bandit
  team hostile
  module f16
  spawn 46.90000 7.70000 8000 270.0 400
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 70
  set datalink off
  set fcr_mode crm
  set store 3 aim120
  set store 7 aim120
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set cmds_mode man
  set cmds_program 1
  set cmds_chaff 60
  set cmds_flare 60
  set task intercept
  wp 46.90000 5.75000 8000 400             # its mirror vector, threat bearing 270
